# Intervals: geom_linerange

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_linerange()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_linerange.html), which covers
`geom_crossbar()`, `geom_errorbar()`, `geom_errorbarh()` and `geom_pointrange()` too, in the order
the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly
cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list
of what remains.

These geoms each draw an interval: a range from `ymin` to `ymax` at an `x`, or from `xmin` to
`xmax` at a `y` when turned on its side. `geom_linerange()` draws it as a line,
`geom_pointrange()` as a line with a point at `y`, `geom_errorbar()` as a line capped at each end,
and `geom_crossbar()` as a box with a line across it at `y`. `geom_errorbarh()` is deprecated in
favour of `geom_errorbar(orientation = "y")`. Giggly has none of them yet.

## Lines, points, crossbars and error bars

```r
# Create a simple example dataset
df <- data.frame(
  trt = factor(c(1, 1, 2, 2)),
  resp = c(1, 5, 3, 4),
  group = factor(c(1, 2, 1, 2)),
  upper = c(1.1, 5.3, 3.3, 4.2),
  lower = c(0.8, 4.6, 2.4, 3.6)
)

p <- ggplot(df, aes(trt, resp, colour = group))
p + geom_linerange(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper))
p + geom_pointrange(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper))
p + geom_crossbar(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), width = 0.2)
p + geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), width = 0.2)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_linerange()`, `geom_pointrange()`, `geom_crossbar()` and `geom_errorbar()`
and their `width` option.

## Horizontal intervals

```r
# Flip the orientation by changing mapping
ggplot(df, aes(resp, trt, colour = group)) +
  geom_linerange(aes(xmin = lower, xmax = upper))
```

Mapping `xmin` and `xmax` rather than `ymin` and `ymax` turns the intervals on their side.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_linerange()`.

## Lines through the group means

```r
# Draw lines connecting group means
p +
  geom_line(aes(group = group)) +
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), width = 0.2)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_errorbar()` and its `width` option.

## Dodging bars and error bars

```r
# If you want to dodge bars and errorbars, you need to manually
# specify the dodge width
p <- ggplot(df, aes(trt, resp, fill = group))
p +
 geom_col(position = "dodge") +
 geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), position = "dodge", width = 0.25)
```

Each layer is dodged by its own width here, so the narrower error bars do not line up with the
bars.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_errorbar()` and its `width` option. Giggly dodges columns already:
`Giggly.Plot.geom_col/3` with `position: :dodge`.

## Dodging by one width

```r
# Because the bars and errorbars have different widths
# we need to specify how wide the objects we are dodging are
dodge <- position_dodge(width=0.9)
p +
  geom_col(position = dodge) +
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), position = dodge, width = 0.25)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `position_dodge()`'s options, and `geom_errorbar()` and its `width` option.
Giggly names a position by an atom, `position: :dodge`, which takes no options.

## Dodging with padding

```r
# When using geom_errorbar() with position_dodge2(), extra padding will be
# needed between the error bars to keep them aligned with the bars.
p +
geom_col(position = "dodge2") +
geom_errorbar(
  aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper),
  position = position_dodge2(width = 0.5, padding = 0.5)
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `position_dodge2()` and its options, and `geom_errorbar()`.